# Favor Durable Solutions Over Expedient Ones

Default to the solution that will hold up long-term. Quick fixes are a rare exception.

## Challenge these impulses
- "Let's do X for now, we can refactor later."
- Shims, feature flags, or env toggles that defer a decision instead of making it.
- Expedient data structures (flat list when a tree fits; strings when enums fit; duplicated state when one source of truth fits).
- The first library that mostly works, instead of the one that actually fits.

"For now" is almost always a tell. "Later" rarely comes.

## When expedience IS right

Only when ALL of these hold: the problem is genuinely scoped (one-off, throwaway, demo tomorrow); the user has said speed matters; and nothing else will grow on top of the shortcut. If unsure, ask.

## How to apply
- When two paths exist, present both but lead with the durable one.
- If you catch yourself writing "for now," "temporary," "quick fix," "workaround," or "placeholder" — stop and reconsider.
